course: dictionary
lesson: 3692
title: Flashcard
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A flashcard is a small card with a word, picture, question or sum on one side and the answer on the other. Look at the front, think of the answer, then turn the card over to check.
explain: Pupils use flashcards to learn spellings, times tables and foreign words. Teachers often flash the card quickly so children answer fast. The example says «Five minutes a day with three to five words, as a game, works better than an hour of flashcards ending in tears.» The clues «as a game» and «ending in tears» show that short, playful practice works better than long practice that upsets children.
explain: The word can also mean a digital flashcard. Digital means it is on a screen, so a digital flashcard is the same idea on an app or computer.
fact: A flashcard is a small card with a question or word on one side and the answer on the other.
fact: «Flashcards» help you learn spellings, times tables, foreign words and facts for tests.
fact: Teachers often «flash» a card quickly, and children answer.
fact: A digital flashcard is the same thing on an app or computer.
fact: You may hear the phrases «make flashcards», «test me with flashcards» and «revision flashcards».
exercise: What is on the two sides of a flashcard? | Only a picture, with no words | Sums that never have answers | A long story on each side | *A question or word on one side and the answer on the other
exercise: Which of these is a common use of flashcards? | *Learning times tables | Writing a long story | Painting a picture | Swimming lessons
exercise: «Five minutes a day with three to five words, as a game, works better than an hour of flashcards ending in tears.» What does «ending in tears» suggest? | Tears are a good way to learn | Flashcards make children happy | *Long practice can upset children | The cards are wet
test: What does a digital flashcard mean? | A card used only for maps | Paper card with a pencil | A card for playing football | *The same idea on an app or computer
test: Which way of using flashcards does the example suggest? | Practise for an hour until everyone cries | *Five minutes a day, as a game | Never use them | Use them only once a year
exam: Why does short daily practice work better than an hour of flashcards, according to the example? | Because an hour is too short to read | Because flashcards must be used only once | *Because short games keep children interested and less upset | Because the cards get bigger
